$DIR/main.p:16:2: cannot pass the function literal to the function literal at main.p:16, which keeps it after returning
    the function literal points into x. x is a local variable of f (declared at
    main.p:15). the function literal at main.p:16 sends a pointer reachable from
    a variable it uses on a channel (main.p:18), so the function literal would
    outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If the function literal at main.p:16 should keep its own copy of x, change
    it to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int]
    from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:20:2: cannot store s in package variable gs, which outlives x
    s points into x. x is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:15). When f
    returns, x's memory is reclaimed, but package variables live for the whole
    program, so package variable gs would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store values instead of views of x, or allocate x and store its owner, an
    owned[int], which moves there:
        gs = new(int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
